       tension_point = 'strict liability for direct seller vs vicarious liability for owner',
       fact_pattern = 'A state statute provides: “The sale of an alcoholic beverage to any person under the age of 21 is a misdemeanor.” Ruth, who was 20 years old but looked older, entered Manna Mart, a privately owned convenience store near a Christian college. She had a very convincing fake driver’s license indicating that she was 24. Ruth picked up a six-pack of hard cider and placed it on the counter. Timothy, the store clerk, examined the driver’s license and rang up the purchase. Both Timothy and Lydia, the owner of Manna Mart, have been charged with violating the state statute. If the court finds both Timothy and Lydia guilty, what standard of liability must the court have interpreted the statute to impose?',
       question_stem = 'A state statute provides: “The sale of an alcoholic beverage to any person under the age of 21 is a misdemeanor.” Ruth, who was 20 years old but looked older, entered Manna Mart, a privately owned convenience store near a Christian college. She had a very convincing fake driver’s license indicating that she was 24. Ruth picked up a six-pack of hard cider and placed it on the counter. Timothy, the store clerk, examined the driver’s license and rang up the purchase. Both Timothy and Lydia, the owner of Manna Mart, have been charged with violating the state statute. If the court finds both Timothy and Lydia guilty, what standard of liability must the court have interpreted the statute to impose?

If the court finds both Timothy and Lydia guilty, what standard of liability must the court have interpreted the statute to impose?',
