ine applies when the defendant’s intent toward one victim attaches to the defendant’s unintended actual victim","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIM-BASIS-ARRAY-01","statement":"In a guilty/not-guilty because-array, the because-clause is part of the answer. A correct result with the wrong basis is still a trap.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"two guilty answers with different bases","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"answer_array","target_skill":"correct result vs correct basis","prompt":"Two answers say guilty. One says extreme recklessness; one says transferred intent. Another actor fired the fatal shot. Which basis survives?","answer":"Extreme recklessness."},{"drill_type":"gold_key","target_skill":"depraved-heart causation","prompt":"A defendant sends an armed, volatile person into a guarded place. A guard foreseeably kills him. What route to murder is tested?","answer":"Extreme recklessness plus foreseeable causation."},{"drill_type":"wrong_answer_recovery","target_skill":"shooter myth","prompt":"Why is ''not guilty because he did not shoot'' not enough?","answer":"Because murder liability can rest on causing the death with the required mental state."}],"updated_copy_source":"C:/FOC/Workspace/Finished/*_updated.md"}'
