       fact_pattern = 'At common law, first-degree murder is defined as murder committed (1) by means of poison, or (2) with premeditation and deliberation. All other murder is second-degree murder. Manslaughter is defined as at common law.

Hannah is a church custodian. She has worked at the church for years, but the new youth pastor, Timothy, recently criticized her cleaning in front of the congregation. Humiliated and angry, Hannah decided to teach Timothy a lesson. She went to a pharmacy and bought a bottle of non-prescription sleep-aid tablets. That Sunday, while preparing the fellowship-hall coffee before services, Hannah crushed two tablets and stirred them into Timothy''s mug. She intended merely to make Timothy drowsy during the service so he would embarrass himself in front of the church. Timothy drank the coffee. Because Timothy was already taking a prescribed medication that interacted with the sleep aids, he lost consciousness, struck his head on a table, and died from the head injury.



A. A: Yes, only because she acted with premeditation and deliberation.
B. B: only because she used poison.
C. C: No.
D. D: Yes, both because she used poison and because she acted with premeditation and deliberation.',
       question_stem = 'At common law, first-degree murder is defined as murder committed (1) by means of poison, or (2) with premeditation and deliberation. All other murder is second-degree murder. Manslaughter is defined as at common law.

Hannah is a church custodian. She has worked at the church for years, but the new youth pastor, Timothy, recently criticized her cleaning in front of the congregation. Humiliated and angry, Hannah decided to teach Timothy a lesson. She went to a pharmacy and bought a bottle of non-prescription sleep-aid tablets. That Sunday, while preparing the fellowship-hall coffee before services, Hannah crushed two tablets and stirred them into Timothy''s mug. She intended merely to make Timothy drowsy during the service so he would embarrass himself in front of the church. Timothy drank the coffee. Because Timothy was already taking a prescribed medication that interacted with the sleep aids, he lost consciousness, struck his head on a table, and died from the head injury.



A. A: Yes, only because she acted with premeditation and deliberation.
B. B: only because she used poison.
C. C: No.
D. D: Yes, both because she used poison and because she acted with premeditation and deliberation.

If the jurisdiction charges Hannah with first-degree murder, should she be convicted?',
       call_of_question = 'If the jurisdiction charges Hannah with first-degree murder, should she be convicted?',
       difficulty = 2,
       metadata = '{"source_file":"CQ14668.md","source_question_id":"14668","variant_slug":"laxative-lies","batch":"cq-updated-copy-2026-06-20","outline_code":"74020101","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"core","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"HARD_STRUCTURAL","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A person secretly adds a non-lethal substance to another''s drink intending only minor harm/embarrassment. The victim dies from an unforeseen medical interaction. Charged with first-degree murder (poison or premeditation). Guilty?","review_truth":"No malice aforethought means no murder — and without murder, first-degree murder cannot stand.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CRIM-HOMICIDE-01","title":"First-Degree Murder Requires Murder First","signal":"See a question asking about first-degree murder — always check whether murder exists before analyzing degree.","student_move":"Before evaluating poison or premeditation, ask: does the defendant have malice aforethought? If not, the answer is ''no murder'' regardless of the aggravators.","tiny_rule":"No malice = no murder = no first-degree murder","trap":"Jumping to the degree analysis without confirming the underlying crime","confidence":"high"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CRIM-HOMICIDE-01","statement":"For first-degree murder by premeditation, the defendant must have reflected on and planned the decision to kill — not merely planned the act. Planning to put tablets in coffee equals prank-planning, not murder-planning.","type":"distinction","trigger":"A fact pattern where the defendant planned/thought ahead but the intent was something other than killing","authority":"See CA Bar Exam Content Map, Crim Law § II.A.1.b; CA10 Pattern Jury Instructions (premeditation requires forming intent to kill and being ''fully conscious of that intent'')","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CRIM-HOMICIDE-02","statement":"''Poison'' in first-degree murder statutes means a substance that is noxious or 