UPDATE questions
   SET subject = 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW',
       topic = 'Individual Rights',
       subtopic = 'Equal Protection Clause',
       tension_point = NULL,
       fact_pattern = 'A state vocational rehabilitation program provides paid medical leave for workers injured in program-sponsored training. The program excludes leave for disabilities arising from pregnancy-related complications. An employee brings a federal Equal Protection claim against the exclusion but offers no evidence that the state adopted the exclusion because of sex stereotypes. 

(A) Equal Protection requires every state benefit program to cover all medical conditions without exception.

(B) The exclusion automatically receives intermediate scrutiny because pregnancy is always a sex classification under the Equal Protection Clause.

(C) The Equal Protection claim does not automatically trigger intermediate scrutiny on pregnancy status alone.

(D) Strict scrutiny applies because pregnancy implicates bodily autonomy.',
       question_stem = 'A state vocational rehabilitation program provides paid medical leave for workers injured in program-sponsored training. The program excludes leave for disabilities arising from pregnancy-related complications. An employee brings a federal Equal Protection claim against the exclusion but offers no evidence that the state adopted the exclusion because of sex stereotypes. 

(A) Equal Protection requires every state benefit program to cover all medical conditions without exception.

(B) The exclusion automatically receives intermediate scrutiny because pregnancy is always a sex classification under the Equal Protection Clause.

(C) The Equal Protection claim does not automatically trigger intermediate scrutiny on pregnancy status alone.

(D) Strict scrutiny applies because pregnancy implicates bodily autonomy.

Which statement is most accurate?',
       call_of_question = 'Which statement is most accurate?',
       difficulty = 2,
       metadata = '{"source_file":"CQ17157.md","source_question_id":"17157","variant_slug":"vocational-rehab","batch":"cq-updated-copy-2026-06-20","outline_code":null,"outline_code_verified":false,"difficulty_band":"core","deciding_phase":"CLASH","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A state disability-benefit program excludes pregnancy-related conditions; the plaintiff brings only an Equal Protection claim and offers no proof of sex-stereotyping purpose. What level of scrutiny applies?","review_truth":"A pregnancy-based exclusion from a state benefit program does not automatically constitute a sex classification triggering intermediate scrutiny under the Equal Protection Clause.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"RC-CONLAW-EP-01","title":"Pregnancy ≠ Sex Classification on Constitutional EP","trigger_signal":"State program excludes pregnancy conditions; claim is EP-only; no stereotyping evidence","student_move":"When the stem says ''only an Equal Protection claim'' and ''no evidence of sex stereotypes,'' map to Geduldig: pregnancy classification alone does not trigger intermediate scrutiny. Rational basis applies.","tiny_rule":"Pregnancy-alone ≠ automatic sex classification → rational basis, not intermediate scrutiny. Gold Key: Geduldig distinvid.","trap":"B (automatic intermediate scrutiny)","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-EP-01","statement":"A pregnancy-based classification is not automatically a sex-based classification under the constitutional Equal Protection Clause. (Geduldig v. Aiello, 417 U.S. 484 (1974)). Intermediate scrutiny applies only when the classification is genuinely sex-based or when sex-stereotyping purpose is proven.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Any MBE question where a state program excludes pregnancy conditions and the claim is constitutional EP only (no Title VII / PDA), with no showing of sex-stereotyping purpose","authority":"Geduldig v. Aiello, 417 U.S. 484 (1974)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-EP-01","statement":"When two choices survive the CUT and one says ''always/never'' while the other is hedged (''does not automatically''), look up the Geduldig distinction — the hedged answer is usually correct in EP classification questions.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"EP classification question with a pregnancy or sex-classification tension where one option uses absolute language and the other is qualified","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"classification","target_skill":"EP scrutiny level identification","prompt":"A state university''s health plan excludes coverage for gender-affirming surgical procedures. A student brings an Equal Protection claim. What level of scrutiny applies, and why is this different from Geduldig?","answer":"Gender-affirming care exclusions classify based on transgender status, which the Supreme Court in Bostock (via Title VII) and emerging case law treats as sex-based. This is a sex classification triggering intermediate scrutiny — unlike Geduldig''s pregnancy-alone rule, because the classification is facially sex-based (transgender status = sex-linked), not pregnancy-linked."},{"drill_type":"distinction","target_skill":"Constitutional vs. statutory pregnancy discrimination","prompt":"Explain why Geduldig v. Aiello (1974) reached the opposite result from the Pregnancy Discrimination Act (1978) on the same underlying facts.","answer":"Geduldig held that pregnancy exclusion is not a constitutional sex classification under EP (rational basis applies). Congress then passed the PDA, amending Title VII to statutorily define pregnancy discrimination as sex discrimination. The PDA is a statutory override — it does not change the constitutional rule, but it provides a statutory cause of action that reaches what Geduldig left untouched."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"Absolute vs. hedged language in EP questions","prompt":"You see two choices: (A) ''The classification always triggers intermediate scrutiny'' and (B) ''The classification does not automatically trigger intermediate scrutiny.'' The stem involves a state program excluding a sex-linked condition with no evidence of discriminatory intent. Which do you pick and why?","answer":"Pick B. The word ''always'' is a structural red flag in EP classification questions. Without proof of sex-based purpose or a facial sex classification, the Geduldig rule applies: the classification is reviewed rationally. The hedged ''not automatically'' language signals the accurate statement."}],"updated_copy_source":"C:/FOC/Workspace/Finished/*_updated.md"}'
