       fact_pattern = 'A federal statute provides that, for every federal tax, employee-benefit, grant, and insurance program, the word ''spouse'' means only a husband or wife of the opposite sex, and ''marriage'' means only a legal union between one man and one woman. Stephen and Barnabas, who run a little gospel-poster shop called Loaves & Pages, are legally married under their state’s law. After Stephen takes a federal park-service job, he tries to enroll Barnabas for spousal health coverage available to opposite-sex spouses of federal employees. The agency denies the enrollment solely because Barnabas is not an opposite-sex spouse. Stephen and Barnabas challenge the statute under the Equal Protection component of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause.',
       question_stem = 'A federal statute provides that, for every federal tax, employee-benefit, grant, and insurance program, the word ''spouse'' means only a husband or wife of the opposite sex, and ''marriage'' means only a legal union between one man and one woman. Stephen and Barnabas, who run a little gospel-poster shop called Loaves & Pages, are legally married under their state’s law. After Stephen takes a federal park-service job, he tries to enroll Barnabas for spousal health coverage available to opposite-sex spouses of federal employees. The agency denies the enrollment solely because Barnabas is not an opposite-sex spouse. Stephen and Barnabas challenge the statute under the Equal Protection component of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause.

Under Equal Protection analysis, how should a court evaluate the federal statute’s refusal to recognize their state-law marriage for federal benefits?',
       call_of_question = 'Under Equal Protection analysis, how should a court evaluate the federal statute’s refusal to recognize their state-law marriage for federal benefits?',
       difficulty = 3,
       metadata = '{"source_file":"CQ19194.md","source_question_id":"19194","variant_slug":"loaves_pages","batch":"cq-updated-copy-2026-06-20","outline_code":"44040305","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"hard","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A federal law defines marriage/spouse as opposite-sex only and denies a federal benefit to a same-sex spouse who is lawfully married under state law. Under Equal Protection, does the federal definition survive?","review_truth":"A federal marriage-recognition law cannot deny federal benefits to state-law same-sex spouses when the operative purpose/effect is to demean their lawful marriage status.","dominant_trap":"B","anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-CONLAW-WINDSOR-BARE-ANIMUS","title":"Federal benefits + state-law same-sex marriage","signal":"Federal law refuses to recognize a same-sex spouse whom state law recognizes.","student_move":"Lock the Equal Protection call; avoid both strict-scrutiny overpromotion and benefits-entitlement wrong frame.","tiny_rule":"Bare animus/equal-dignity injury is not a legitimate interest.","trap":"Rational-basis deference plus tradition language.","confidence":"PURE_ANCHOR"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CONLAW-WINDSOR-DOMA-01","statement":"When a federal marriage-recognition law denies federal benefits to same-sex spouses who are lawfully married under state law for the purpose/effect of marking those marriages as lesser, it fails Fifth Amendment equal-protection principles; bare animus is not a legitimate interest.","type":"constitutional_principle","trigger":"Federal definition of spouse/marriage excludes state-law same-sex spouses from federal benefits.","authority":"United States v. Windsor, 570 U.S. 744 (2013); Romer v. Evans, 517 U.S. 620 (1996).","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-CONLAW-WINDSOR-SCRUTINY-02","statement":"Do not turn Windsor into a strict-scrutiny/suspect-classification answer; Windsor invalidated DOMA without expressly making sexual orientation a suspect classification.","type":"distinction","trigger":"Answer says sexual orientation is a suspect classification under Windsor-style facts.","authority":"United States v. Windsor, 570 U.S. 744 (2013).","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CONLAW-BENEFIT-FRAME-01","statement":"When the call is Equal Protection, do not answer whether the benefit exists as a free-standing right; answer whether the government may draw this line.","type":"call_focus","trigger":"Answer talks about Congress’s benefits power or no constitutional right to a benefit.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"Gold Key recall","target_skill":"Windsor/Romer bare animus","prompt":"Federal law denies federal recognition to a same-sex spouse lawfully married under state law. What tiny anchor decides the item?","answer":"Bare animus/equal-dignity injury is not a legitimate governmental interest; Windsor/Romer controls."},{"drill_type":"Silver Key call-focus","target_skill":"wrong-frame cut","prompt":"The call asks Equal Protection, but one answer says Congress controls federal benefits. What is the cut?","answer":"NOT_RESPONSIVE misfit: benefits authority does not answer whether the classification violates Equal Protection."},{"drill_type":"Strict-scrutiny trap","target_skill":"tier calibration","prompt":"An answer says Windsor requires strict scrutiny because sexual orientation is a suspect class. What is the recovery move?","answer":"Do not overpromote Windsor; it invalidated DOMA without expressly declaring suspect-class status."}],"updated_copy_source":"C:/FOC/Workspace/Finished/*_updated.md"}'
