UPDATE answer_choices
   SET choice_text = 'The statute fails Equal Protection review because denying federal recognition to lawfully married same-sex couples serves only to demean their status, and a bare desire to harm an identifiable group is not a legitimate governmental interest under any level of scrutiny.',
       is_correct = 1,
       forensic_tags = '[]',
       misconception_tags = '[]',
       why_attractive = NULL,
       why_wrong_or_correct = 'D is the best answer. Gold Key: A federal marriage-recognition law that denies federal benefits to same-sex spouses who are lawfully married under state law fails when its purpose and effect are to impose a separate, lesser status on those marriages. Silver Key: Do not let “Congress controls benefits” or “no one has a right to a benefit” answer the wrong question. The call is Equal Protection: why did the government draw this line?

The statute denies federal recognition to a marriage the state has made lawful. The answer does not need to promote sexual orientation to suspect-class status. It also does not ask whether federal benefits exist as a free-standing right. It asks whether this classification may be justified. Under the controlling Windsor/Romer line, a bare desire to demean or injure an identifiable group is not a legitimate governmental interest.',
       future_cue = NULL,
       remediation_id = 'REM-CONLAW-WINDSOR-BARE-ANIMUS'
