What standard should the court apply?',
       call_of_question = 'What standard should the court apply?',
       difficulty = 2,
       metadata = '{"source_file":"CQ19266.md","source_question_id":"19266","variant_slug":"carol_banquet_jmol","batch":"cq-updated-copy-2026-06-20","outline_code":"92080201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":"CUT","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"After a plaintiff has been fully heard at trial and has no evidence on a required element, what Rule 50 standard governs JMOL?","review_truth":"Rule 50 JMOL turns on whether a reasonable jury has a legally sufficient evidentiary basis on the targeted issue, not sympathy, bad faith, or automatic jury submission.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"CIV-REM-JMOL-EVIDENCE-GATE-01","title":"JMOL = evidence gate, not moral gate","signal":"Party rests; motion targets one element.","student_move":"Ask whether the record contains evidence a reasonable jury could use on that element.","tiny_rule":"No evidence on an essential element means no legally sufficient jury basis.","trap":"Bad faith, sympathy, or jury-autopilot standards.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JMOL-STANDARD-01","statement":"JMOL asks whether, after a party has been fully heard on an issue, a reasonable jury would have a legally sufficient evidentiary basis to find for that party on that issue.","type":"threshold","trigger":"Party rests; opponent moves for JMOL on a specific element.","authority":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a)(1)","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CIVIL_PROCEDURE-JMOL-PROCEDURAL-LENS-01","statement":"On a JMOL motion, stay on trial evidence for the targeted element; do not drift to sympathy, filing conduct, or automatic jury submission.","type":"trap_spotting","trigger":"Call asks what standard the court should apply to JMOL.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"procedural_frame","prompt":"A plaintiff rests with no evidence on causation. Defendant moves for JMOL. Cut an answer saying JMOL turns on plaintiff bad faith.","answer":"Cut as fabricated_rule / procedural_frame."},{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"A choice says JMOL must be denied automatically because all civil trials go to the jury. What is the mold?","answer":"tiered_absolute."},{"drill_type":"anchor","target_skill":"Rule 50 standard","prompt":"State the JMOL standard after a party has been fully heard on an issue.","answer":"Whether a reasonable jury would have a legally sufficient evidentiary basis to find for that party on that issue."},{"drill_type":"trap_spotting","target_skill":"sympathy vs evidence","prompt":"A judge thinks the plaintiff is sympathetic, but the record has no evidence on an element. Does sympathy supply a jury basis?","answer":"No."}],"updated_copy_source":"C:/FOC/Workspace/Finished/*_updated.md"}'
