   SET subject = 'TORTS',
       topic = 'Negligence damages',
       subtopic = 'Negligent misrepresentation and economic loss',
       tension_point = 'open-ended foreseeability vs targeted-use duty',
       fact_pattern = 'At a for-profit Bible-camp supply expo, Martha sold off the remaining stock from her “Loaves & Fishes” craft-kit shop. Weeks earlier, Lydia, a professional inventory consultant, had prepared a crate-count sheet solely so Martha could plan warehouse space and packing for the expo. The sheet mistakenly listed 900 sealed craft kits instead of 90. Lydia was not told that the sheet would be shown to buyers or used in any sale. Martha later slipped a copy of the sheet into a sale packet on her booth table. Timothy, who had never dealt with Lydia, used the sheet to buy the remaining kits for his own resale booth and lost money when the count proved wrong.

In Timothy’s negligent-misrepresentation claim against Lydia, how should the court rule?',
       question_stem = 'At a for-profit Bible-camp supply expo, Martha sold off the remaining stock from her “Loaves & Fishes” craft-kit shop. Weeks earlier, Lydia, a professional inventory consultant, had prepared a crate-count sheet solely so Martha could plan warehouse space and packing for the expo. The sheet mistakenly listed 900 sealed craft kits instead of 90. Lydia was not told that the sheet would be shown to buyers or used in any sale. Martha later slipped a copy of the sheet into a sale packet on her booth table. Timothy, who had never dealt with Lydia, used the sheet to buy the remaining kits for his own resale booth and lost money when the count proved wrong.

In Timothy’s negligent-misrepresentation claim against Lydia, how should the court rule?

Prediction: rule for Lydia. Timothy is outside the targeted group and transaction for which Lydia supplied the count sheet.

Adjacent-call trap: do not answer whether Timothy relied, lost money, or may have a claim against Martha.',
       call_of_question = 'Prediction: rule for Lydia. Timothy is outside the targeted group and transaction for which Lydia supplied the count sheet.

Adjacent-call trap: do not answer whether Timothy relied, lost money, or may have a claim against Martha.',
       difficulty = 2,
       metadata = '{"source_file":"CQ20024.md","source_question_id":"20024","variant_slug":"loaves_fishes_crate_count","batch":"cq-updated-copy-2026-06-20","outline_code":"62050200","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":null,"confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":"The count sheet was made only for Martha''s internal planning, and Lydia was not told it would be used for buyers or any sale.","distilled_core_question":"A professional prepared an inaccurate count sheet only for a client’s internal planning. An unknown later buyer relied on a copy in a sale packet and lost money. Is the professional liable to that buyer for negligent misrepresentation?","review_truth":"For negligent misrepresentation causing economic loss, do not stop at foreseeable reader; ask whether the plaintiff was in the known or limited group for the transaction the information was meant to guide.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-NMISREP-01","title":"Foreseeability is not the whole duty gate.","signal":"Professional information + economic loss + unknown later reader.","student_move":"Ask: targeted person/class and targeted transaction?","tiny_rule":"Foreseeability alone loses to targeted-use duty.","trap":"General negligence reflex.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-LIMITED-CLASS-01","statement":"For negligent misrepresentation by a professional information supplier, economic-loss duty is limited to a known person or limited group for a specific transaction or substantially similar transaction. It does not run to every foreseeable later reader.","type":"threshold","trigger":"professional report, economic loss, unknown third-party reader","authority":"Restatement (Second) of Torts § 552; Bily v. Arthur Young & Co.; Ultramares Corp. v. Touche.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-TARGETED-USE-01","statement":"When one finalist says ''foreseeable reader'' and another says ''known or limited class for a transaction,'' name the axis as targeted-use duty, not general foreseeability.","type":"clash","trigger":"unknown later reader relying on a client-republished report","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"overclaim","prompt":"No, because pure economic loss is never recoverable for negligent misrepresentation.","answer":"CUT: tiered_absolute / NOT_TRUE."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"targeted-use axis","prompt":"Foreseeable later reader vs known/limited class for a transaction. Which fact decides?","answer":"Whether the professional supplied the information for that person/class and transaction."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"Gold Key recall","prompt":"Professional report, economic loss, unknown third-party reader. What tiny rule?","answer":"Duty is limited to a known or limited group for a specific transaction; foreseeability alone is not enough."}],"updated_copy_source":"C:/FOC/Workspace/Finished/*_updated.md"}'
