       tension_point = 'notice-after-acceptance vs defect-alone defense',
       fact_pattern = 'Paul, a devotional-merchandise wholesaler, sued Lydia on August 9 for the price of 360 ceramic “Faith, Hope, Love” mugs. The sale began on March 7, when Lydia, who operates a Christian gift shop, ordered mugs finished with a gold dove decal, for delivery by March 18. On March 18, the mugs reached Lydia’s shop. Timothy, Lydia’s stockroom manager, opened the cartons and told Lydia both that the order had arrived and that the mugs were missing the gold dove decals. Lydia meant to text Paul that afternoon, but while preparing a children’s choir sale she forgot. On April 27, Lydia received Paul’s invoice but neither paid nor contacted him. In defense to Paul’s action, Lydia asserted that the mugs did not conform to the contract of sale.',
       question_stem = 'Paul, a devotional-merchandise wholesaler, sued Lydia on August 9 for the price of 360 ceramic “Faith, Hope, Love” mugs. The sale began on March 7, when Lydia, who operates a Christian gift shop, ordered mugs finished with a gold dove decal, for delivery by March 18. On March 18, the mugs reached Lydia’s shop. Timothy, Lydia’s stockroom manager, opened the cartons and told Lydia both that the order had arrived and that the mugs were missing the gold dove decals. Lydia meant to text Paul that afternoon, but while preparing a children’s choir sale she forgot. On April 27, Lydia received Paul’s invoice but neither paid nor contacted him. In defense to Paul’s action, Lydia asserted that the mugs did not conform to the contract of sale.

The court should rule for',
