       fact_pattern = 'Lydia owned Selah Studio, a private Bible-verse calligraphy studio open to the public for paid lettering workshops and sales of framed Scripture cards. After closing, Lydia hired Barnabas to install a motorized “Jericho Wall” display in the front room. When a participant stepped on a pressure mat, wooden panels were supposed to slide apart to reveal the day’s featured verse.

Barnabas read the manufacturer’s instructions but disregarded the warnings. When he finished, he did not test the display by stepping on the pressure mat, but told Ruth, Lydia’s night manager, that the installation was complete. The next morning, when Selah Studio opened, Mary entered and was struck by one of the moving panels. Mary asserted a claim for her injuries against Lydia.

Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it was the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Mary?',
       question_stem = 'Lydia owned Selah Studio, a private Bible-verse calligraphy studio open to the public for paid lettering workshops and sales of framed Scripture cards. After closing, Lydia hired Barnabas to install a motorized “Jericho Wall” display in the front room. When a participant stepped on a pressure mat, wooden panels were supposed to slide apart to reveal the day’s featured verse.

