       tension_point = 'fact mistake versus law mistake',
       fact_pattern = 'At a community Advent craft swap, Stephen sees a hand-carved olivewood nativity figurine on a table beside small giveaway cards printed with Bible verses. A sign on the table says, “Free verse cards—take one.” Stephen honestly believes the figurine itself has been left as part of the giveaway and takes it home intending to keep it. The figurine actually belongs to Ruth, who had set it down briefly while stacking programs. Stephen is charged with larceny, a specific intent crime requiring intent to permanently deprive another of property.

Later, at the same event, Mary sees Paul set down a similar olivewood figurine while he helps lead the closing song. Mary knows the figurine belongs to someone at the event, but she mistakenly believes state law permits a person to keep any item left unattended on a public craft-swap table after the closing song. Mary takes it intending to keep it. Mary is also charged with larceny; her belief about the law was wrong.',
       question_stem = 'At a community Advent craft swap, Stephen sees a hand-carved olivewood nativity figurine on a table beside small giveaway cards printed with Bible verses. A sign on the table says, “Free verse cards—take one.” Stephen honestly believes the figurine itself has been left as part of the giveaway and takes it home intending to keep it. The figurine actually belongs to Ruth, who had set it down briefly while stacking programs. Stephen is charged with larceny, a specific intent crime requiring intent to permanently deprive another of property.

Later, at the same event, Mary sees Paul set down a similar olivewood figurine while he helps lead the closing song. Mary knows the figurine belongs to someone at the event, but she mistakenly believes state law permits a person to keep any item left unattended on a public craft-swap table after the closing song. Mary takes it intending to keep it. Mary is also charged with larceny; her belief about the law was wrong.

