w summary.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-CRIMINAL-FACT-LAW-MATRIX-01","statement":"Read the answer array as two switches: Stephen''s mistake is about a fact; Mary''s mistake is about the law. The credited answer must split them, not treat both defendants the same.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Two scenarios plus choices that say both guilty, both not guilty, and two split answers.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"matrix_read","target_skill":"answer_array","prompt":"One defendant mistakenly thinks property is free; another knows it belongs to someone but misunderstands the law. Which outcome pair should you expect?","answer":"Fact-mistake defendant not guilty if intent is negated; law-mistake defendant guilty."},{"drill_type":"gold_key_recall","target_skill":"anchor","prompt":"For a specific-intent crime, must an honest fact mistake be reasonable?","answer":"No. It must honestly negate the specific intent."},{"drill_type":"trap_cut","target_skill":"wrong_frame","prompt":"Cut: ''Both are guilty because only reasonable mistakes count.''","answer":"Wrong frame. Specific-intent fact mistake does not require reasonableness."}],"updated_copy_source":"C:/FOC/Workspace/Finished/*_updated.md"}'
