   SET subject = 'TORTS',
       topic = 'Negligence',
       subtopic = 'Standard of Due Care / Contributory Negligence / Causation',
       tension_point = 'defendant no-breach versus plaintiff avoidability versus other-cause blame',
       fact_pattern = 'Original row used: BARMATRIX Q# 20649, Torts, official key A. The source row asks which added fact would most likely produce judgment for an independent contractor in an all-or-nothing contributory-negligence jurisdiction. 

Chosen variation title: *The Christmas Pageant Platform*

Naomi worked for Lydia’s private pageant-production company, which was staging a Christian Christmas pageant. The company hired Barnabas, an independent contractor, to adjust the idle setting on a motorized rolling platform that carried a large manger prop. Barnabas adjusted the idle setting too high. Later, Naomi stood beside the platform and pressed the start switch to move it; because the platform lacked a roll-stop interlock, it lurched and injured her.

Naomi asserted a negligence claim against Barnabas. Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Barnabas in a jurisdiction that applies the all-or-nothing rule of contributory negligence?',
       question_stem = 'Original row used: BARMATRIX Q# 20649, Torts, official key A. The source row asks which added fact would most likely produce judgment for an independent contractor in an all-or-nothing contributory-negligence jurisdiction. 

Chosen variation title: *The Christmas Pageant Platform*

Naomi worked for Lydia’s private pageant-production company, which was staging a Christian Christmas pageant. The company hired Barnabas, an independent contractor, to adjust the idle setting on a motorized rolling platform that carried a large manger prop. Barnabas adjusted the idle setting too high. Later, Naomi stood beside the platform and pressed the start switch to move it; because the platform lacked a roll-stop interlock, it lurched and injured her.

Naomi asserted a negligence claim against Barnabas. Which one of the following additional facts or inferences, if it were the only one true, would be most likely to result in a judgment for Barnabas in a jurisdiction that applies the all-or-nothing rule of contributory negligence?

Prediction: pick the fact that attacks Barnabas’s own negligence. Best target: unforeseeable risk or no breach.

Adjacent-call traps:

A and D answer “who else is at fault?”

B answers “could Naomi have avoided injury?”

The real call asks “what fact helps Barnabas win?”',
       call_of_question = 'Prediction: pick the fact that attacks Barnabas’s own negligence. Best target: unforeseeable risk or no breach.

Adjacent-call traps:

A and D answer “who else is at fault?”

B answers “could Naomi have avoided injury?”

The real call asks “what fact helps Barnabas win?”',
       difficulty = 2,
       metadata = '{"source_file":"CQ20649.md","source_question_id":"20649","variant_slug":"christmas_pageant_platform","batch":"cq-updated-copy-2026-06-20","outline_code":"61020201","outline_code_verified":true,"difficulty_band":"medium","deciding_phase":null,"confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED","splitting_fact":null,"distilled_core_question":"A contractor adjusted equipment in a way that caused injury only because a safety device was missing. Which added fact most helps the contractor win: normal safety-device custom, plaintiff avoidability, product defect, or another party''s notice failure?","review_truth":"A defendant wins a negligence claim by breaking the plaintiff''s proof of the defendant''s own unreasonable foreseeable-risk conduct, not by merely pointing to avoidability or another possible cause.","dominant_trap":null,"anchor_card":{"card_id":"REM-TORTS-20649-01","title":"Avoidability is not contributory negligence","signal":"The answer says the plaintiff would not have been injured if she had acted differently.","student_move":"Ask whether the actual conduct is described as unreasonable.","tiny_rule":"Contributory negligence needs plaintiff negligence, not mere avoidability.","trap":"Safer hypothetical sold as all-or-nothing plaintiff fault.","confidence":"ANCHOR_ASSISTED"},"gold_keys":[{"id":"GK-TORTS-FORESEEABLE-RISK-01","statement":"Negligence turns on unreasonable conduct in light of a foreseeable risk. A fact that makes the specific risk not foreseeable can defeat breach.","type":"rule","trigger":"The added fact says the missing safety device is usually present.","authority":"Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 3.","last_minute_review":true},{"id":"GK-TORTS-CONTRIB-AVOIDABILITY-01","statement":"Contributory negligence requires negligent plaintiff conduct that contributed to the harm. A fact that the plaintiff would have avoided injury by doing something else is not enough by itself.","type":"distinction","trigger":"The answer says the plaintiff would not have been hurt from another position.","authority":"Common-law contributory negligence rule.","last_minute_review":true}],"silver_keys":[{"id":"SK-TORTS-PAGEANT-ARRAY-01","statement":"In an added-fact defense question, sort each option by what it attacks: defendant breach, plaintiff negligence, other cause, or other party.","type":"answer_array","trigger":"Choices point in four different blame directions.","authority":null,"last_minute_review":true}],"drill_seeds":[{"drill_type":"CUT","target_skill":"wrong-element","prompt":"A plaintiff would not have been injured if she had used a safer position. Does that alone prove contributory negligence?","answer":"No. It shows avoidability, not negligent conduct."},{"drill_type":"CLASH","target_skill":"tension axis","prompt":"Two answers survive: one says the device is usually present; one says the plaintiff could have stood elsewhere. Name the axis.","answer":"Defendant no-breach / unforeseeable risk versus plaintiff avoidability."},{"drill_type":"CALL","target_skill":"answer-array sorting","prompt":"In an added-fact defense call, which fact is stronger: one blaming a manufacturer, or one making the defendant''s risk unforeseeable?","answer":"The unforeseeable-risk fact, because it attacks the plaintiff''s claim against this defendant."},{"drill_type":"ANCHOR","target_skill":"contributory negligence","prompt":"What must the added fact show before contributory negligence bars recovery?","answer":"Plaintiff negligence that contributed to the harm."}],"updated_copy_source":"C:/FOC/Workspace/Finished/*_updated.md"}'
